Scott

On Sunday, November 24, 2013 4:19:28 PM UTC-8, Andy Kipp wrote:
> Alok,
>
> As you pointed out, using SQLite may be a good option. However, I wanted to bring up a few points which are outlined in the Application Instances <https://www.google.com/url?q=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Frstudio%2Fshinyapps%2Fblob%2Fmaster%2Fguide%2Fguide.md%23application-instances&sa=D&sntz=1&usg=AFQjCNEzblxtrEjDr3Fs67HDTEJrcvif8A> section of the Getting Started Guide:
>
> Files that are written to the local file system (such as SQLite database) of a running application are not persisted across deployments or instance restarts. In other words, if you were to re-deploy the application, the files that were written before you re-deployed, would be lost. Additionally, sometimes application instances may need to restarted (to either balance load across our servers, or to recover from a server failure). In this case the files written locally would be also lost.
>
> If you were to use SQLite in a readonly manner, this would not be an issue.
>
> Additionally, it's worth noting that shiny-server (which ShinyApps uses internally) does create multiple R processes to serve requests to an application. While its possible for multiple processes to open a SQLite database, only one process may write to it.
>
>
> On Sun, Nov 24, 2013 at 8:31 AM, Alok Nayak <alokn...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi Scott,
> > I am from python background and I think one can work with "SQLite" database without server support. In R theres a sqlite package <http://www.google.com/url?q=http%3A%2F%2Fcran.r-project.org%2Fweb%2Fpackages%2FRSQLite%2Findex.html&sa=D&sntz=1&usg=AFQjCNGI3VisoMVXS4vGjF6ritqavI2tWw> . you can use this package for interfacing with a database file, placed in your app directory. Let me know, I can dig deeper in this and test it
> >
> >
> > On Wednesday, November 20, 2013 11:42:41 AM UTC+5:30, Scott Chamberlain wrote:
> > > Hi, I'd like to deploy an app with a MySQL backend.  Can that be done on Shinyapps.io?  If not with MySQL, with any other SQL or NoSQL backend?
> > >
> > > Scott
